  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on skewness, defined as assigning more probability to unfavorable (i.e., negative) outcomes, and prefers the more right-skewed lottery. In this case, Lottery A is more right-skewed (90% on loss) than B (45% on loss), so the reason would select A. The individual also selects A, but their justification is based on risk aversion and avoiding a potentially double-sized loss, not on a deliberate comparison of the skewness or distributional shape. They emphasize the 'almost certain' loss in A as preferable to the 'risk' in B, which aligns more with a risk-averse or loss-aversion heuristic than with a formal consideration of skewness. While both the reason and the individual prefer A, the individual does not mention or appear to reason about the overall shape of the distribution or the concept of skewness. Instead, they focus on outcome magnitude and uncertainty, particularly the worst-case scenario. Therefore, the reasoning process described does not clearly reflect the skewness-based rule, even though the choice aligns with it.",
  "final_assessment_score": 30
